
		Late Binding Package for Forth-83
		     ---------------------
	This package, written on a Sun workstation using Mitch Bradley's PD
F83 changes Forth from an early binding language to a late binding language
(like Lisp and Smalltalk). This change has its drawbacks, the resulting code
is less efficient, and continued use of the late binding will eventually
cause you to run out of memory (unlike Lisp and Smalltalk, this package does
NOT handle garbage collection).
	So....if it's inefficient (both n terms of speed and memory usage), 
why use it? When developing Forth code, its annoying to have to reload a slew
of words after slightly changing a low level word deep in the bowels of
your code. This package allows you to change a low level word, and have
all of the higher level words which use that word use the new definition.
	Once the program is working, just forget the late binding words, and
recompile with the normal Forth compiler and the package will be in standard,
early binding Forth.
	Words added:
		examine
			examine is a version of the word see (the
			decompiler in the Forth for which this was written)
			and uses the see subword (see. This decompiles
			late-binding words, which see does not.
	Words redefined for late binding:
		:
		;
		constant
	All of these words function identically to their early-binding
	counterparts (and in fact, with the exception of constant, they use
	their early binding counterparts), but they produce late-binding words.
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
( I hope no one is using a Hazeltine )
			Implementation method
	What this package does, and how it does it
	: is redefined. The word to be compiled is created using make, and
then the word is changed (inside the tib) to an underlying word (where the
actual code is compiled), using word>underlying-word. (Note: if the word 
already existed...it is not redefined by make...). It is this underlying word
which is compiled using the standard early-binding definition of :.
	; first calls the early binding version of ; to finish the underlying
word. It then uses does to reassign the top-level word to do the underlying
word. Once this is done it hides the underlying word.
	Esentially, this is standard Forth forward referencing/defered
words, but with all of the "paper work" handled by the new versions of :
and ;.
	constant is a simple redefinition. If the constant already
exists, then the pfa is changed, otherwise it is created just like a
normal constant.
	Nonstandard words used in this package:
"" - an immediate word which takes the next blank delimited string from the
input stream, puts it in a pack string temporary location, and leaves the
address of that string on the stack.
"variable - defining word which defines a packed array of characters n bytes
long (n being on the stack when "variable is executed)
"copy - takes two addresses of strings ( addr1 addr2 -- ), and copies addr1
to addr2
"create - takes a pointer to a packed array of characters, and creates
a word with that name
hide - this Forth uses two words, hide and reveal, in the place of the
toggeled smudge. If your Forth uses smudge, replace hide (hide works only
on the most recently defined word)
